# SRG-APP-000375-WSR-000171  -  CAT II
# The web server must record time stamps for log records to a minimum
# granularity of one second.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000375-WSR-000171
group:           V-206426
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001889
control_rev5:    AU-8

contribution:    Splunk Web is a component of the application, so this requirement falls
                 inside the application boundary; the application names the access log and
                 sets the strptime format applied to it when the record is indexed.

governs:         props.conf - How incoming data is recognized, parsed and timestamped.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           props.conf [splunk_web_access] TIME_FORMAT matches %S
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] log.access_file https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] log.access_file non_empty
                 reason: "log.access_file = <filename>. The HTTP access log filename. This
                         file is written in the default $SPLUNK_HOME/var/log directory.
                         Default: web_access.log" Related shipped settings:
                         log.access_maxsize = 25000000 and log.access_maxfiles = 5.
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The layout of the access record Splunk Web writes is fixed by the product
                 and is not a configurable setting, so the granularity present in the file
                 itself is a product property rather than something configuration can raise
                 or lower.
external:        Host clock resolution and synchronization to an authoritative source are
                 operating system and time service functions.

citation:        web.conf.spec:16 [settings], :871 log.access_file; props.conf.spec:554
                 TIME_FORMAT, :684-700 ADD_EXTRA_TIME_FIELDS default true. No condition is
                 written over ADD_EXTRA_TIME_FIELDS, because "none" still returns a
                 second-granularity _time per props.conf.spec:690-694 and so meets this
                 requirement's floor.
finding:         Assessed at the application layer alone: Splunk Web names no access log,
                 or the TIME_FORMAT set for its source type reads no seconds field, so its
                 log records do not carry a timestamp to one-second granularity.
